{
  "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
  "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 12:32 p.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Section 3 unambiguously mandates a stay pending arbitration, and the FAA's plain text, structure, and purpose confirm that conclusion. Congress directed that a court shall stay the trial of the action until the arbitration is complete. There is no mention of dismissal, and there are no exceptions for cases where all claims are subject to arbitration."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Mr. Geyser, what difference does it make to grant a stay here or dismissal without prejudice?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "When Congress directed courts to stay the trial of a case in deference to arbitration, it meant stop the litigation in court. It did not mean you must retain jurisdiction. It did not mean never dismiss, no matter how clear it is that the case will never come back to court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: So what's unclear about it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ROSENKRANZ: Well, so when Congress used the word \"stay\" back in 1925, it meant that it was requiring courts to stop the litigation, and it understood that courts could achieve it by either retaining jurisdiction over the case and putting it on ice or by dismissing it with -- without prejudice to come back if there's ever something for the court to do."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Have there been any problems in the six --six circuits that have filed -- followed your friend's rule?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ROSENKRANZ: So -- so, yes, Your Honor. The -- the problems in those circuits is that the courts are required to keep these cases on their dockets. And when you look at the differential costs to the district courts itself as opposed to -- to the parties, this is -- if you nationalize this, this is death by tens of thousands of cuts."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Jackson",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "8a950433877af8b20ab809412c984d5772126dcfb8c9b6936611a92646518d8f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-8a950433877a-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE JACKSON: Is there a rule that the district court has to hold a status conference? I was not aware of that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2023-22-1218",
